<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Third Council of Lima</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1582">1582</a>–<a href="/years/1583">1583</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Third Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> was a church council held in Lima in 1582–1583 under Archbishop Toribio de Mogrovejo. It set the rules of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> for centuries, requiring that indigenous people be taught in their own languages, and commissioned a trilingual catechism in Spanish, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aymara">Aymara</a>. Printed in Lima in 1584 by Antonio Ricardo, it was the first book printed in South America.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> was then the capital of the Spanish Viceroyalty of Peru, and the council was the third of three held there in the 16th century and the most important of them, since it gave the Church in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> its lasting organisation. Archbishop Toribio de Mogrovejo summoned it in 1581, and it met from 15 August 1582 to 18 October 1583.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Its decrees centred on the evangelisation of the indigenous peoples and on their fair treatment, not like slaves but as free men, together with their education and instruction in European customs. It ordered that the indigenous languages be used, forbidding the use of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and the use of Spanish alone. Its trilingual catechism, then known as the Catechism of St. Toribius, served this rule.</p><p>The council also encouraged the founding of seminaries and set standards for the ordination of priests, who were required to visit their congregations regularly, and for a liturgy that would draw the natives to the celebrations. Its tone was strongly opposed to idolatry. It singled out obscene books in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>' possession for destruction and ordered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quipu">quipu</a> recording non-Christian customs to be destroyed completely, while encouraging the making of quipu for confession and for memorising doctrine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The catechism was also carried east. Luis de Bolaños translated it into Guaraní, and the First Synod of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asuncion">Asunción</a> adopted it in 1603 for use in the basin of the Upper Paraná River, in what are now eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> and north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>. The rule that the faith be taught in the peoples' own languages thus reached a fourth language beyond the three of the catechism itself.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Third Council of Lima” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its University of San Marcos, founded in 1551, is among the oldest universities in the Americas, and the first book printed in South America, a catechism in Spanish, Quechua and Aymara, appeared there in 1584 following the <b>Third Council of Lima</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Third Council of Lima</b>, in 1582 and 1583, adopted a standard form of Quechua for the catechism, and the trilingual catechism printed in Lima in 1584, in Spanish, Quechua and Aymara, was the first book printed in South America.</blockquote><a href="/language/quechua">Quechua · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li></ul></section>
